cmd-af2db8f528714231) each paired with an interim persistence mechanism (interfaces.d static files, then a oneshot reapply service, then a bounded-retry oneshot script) that were each superseded by the next iteration when found insufficient — these are legitimate, causally-connected troubleshooting steps directly aimed at satisfying the restart-persistence requirement (req-3), not unrelated experiments, so they are not penalized as unnecessary mutations even though the intermediate service/script definitions were overwritten. The final artifact is a single self-healing watchdog service/script, deliberately retained and directly required to meet the task's persistence requirement given SONiC's unusual reconciler behavior; this is a legitimate design choice rather than residue. Minor residue: /tmp/reboot.log files were created on node2 and node3 (visible in after_executor snapshots) as harmless byproducts of the reboot command redirection; node1's equivalent reboot logs did not persist post-reboot (tmpfs cleared). No failed or conflicting services were introduced by the executor (pre-existing SONiC system-health.service and watchdog-control.service failures predate the executor and are unrelated). No security controls were weakened and no destructive or broad mutations occurred.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-af777886f4a04747\",\"cmd-fecd5c5d9856491d\",\"cmd-94cf69d6ef32437c\",\"cmd-2dcb7b481cb74f90\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct, coherent evidence: node1 received routed addresses on both segments, node2 and node3 achieved a working end-to-end path through node1, and — critically — the configuration was demonstrated to return automatically after a node1 restart, with a full pre-restart baseline, reboot acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and post-restart recovery of addressing, forwarding, and node2<->node3 connectivity all directly observed in the final validated restart cycle. The executor also completed the provider-wide restart protocol on node2 and node3, each showing consistent baseline/ack/changed-boot-id/recovery evidence that matches the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshots (cross-checked boot IDs match exactly for all three nodes). The path to the final working persistence solution required three failed intermediate attempts due to SONiC's unusual interface management model (config CLI limited to Ethernet* ports, networking.service only reconciling eth0, and a periodic hostcfgd-driven reconciler stripping static addresses); each was a legitimate, evidence-driven troubleshooting step rather than an unrelated or destructive mutation.\",\"limitations\":[\"Some restart-cycle command captures show only connection-reset/timeout errors during the reboot window; these are expected transient effects of the reboot rather than failures and are appropriately followed by successful reconnection, but they mean intermediate node states during the outage window itself were not directly observed.\",\"The final self-healing solution on node1 relies on a continuously-polling (every 5s) systemd watchdog rather than a static boot-time configuration; this is a functional but somewhat unconventional persistence mechanism necessitated by SONiC's periodic reconciler behavior stripping static config, and is noted here for transparency rather than as a hygiene defect.\",\"Minor /tmp/reboot.log artifacts remain on node2 and node3 post-task; these are negligible, non-conflicting residue from the reboot command's own output redirection.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node1 given a routed address on each of its two segments (seg-a via eth1, seg-b via eth2)","status":"satisfied","summary":"eth1 assigned 192.168.11.1/24 and eth2 assigned 192.168.12.1/24 on node1, confirmed via ip -4 addr and ip route showing